39: \begin{abstract}
40: \noindent
41: Drag coefficients are reported for three smooth circular cylinders towed through a small towing tank. The models have nominal diameters of \SI{25.4}{mm}, \SI{38.1}{mm} and \SI{50.8}{mm}, and were tested at ten carriage speeds between \SpeedMin{} and \SpeedMax{}~\si{m/s}, giving Reynolds numbers from $\ReMin$ to $\ReMax$. Each record contains a steady window of several seconds, from which the mean drag force is obtained after tare and calibration corrections. Water temperature was measured for every run and used to evaluate density and viscosity, rather than assuming a fixed value. Of the \NTotalRuns{} runs, \NIncludedRuns{} are retained; one run is excluded because the load-cell zero was suspect after the run. The mean drag coefficient over the retained runs is $\MeanCdAll \pm \SdCdAll$ (standard deviation), with cylinder means of \MeanCdOne{}, \MeanCdOneFive{} and \MeanCdTwo{} for the \SI{25.4}{mm}, \SI{38.1}{mm} and \SI{50.8}{mm} models. The differences between cylinders are smaller than the run-to-run scatter, so no resolvable diameter dependence is found over this range. A weak increase of $C_D$ with Reynolds number is observed at the lower end of the range, consistent with the approach to the subcritical plateau. The dominant periodic component of the drag signal scales with carriage speed and diameter and corresponds to a Strouhal number of $\MeanSt \pm \SdSt$, consistent with a drag fluctuation at twice the vortex-shedding frequency. Blockage, finite immersed length and carriage-speed uncertainty are the leading systematic uncertainties and are discussed but not corrected. The raw data, processing code and manuscript source are organised as a reproducible Calkit project.
42: \end{abstract}
43: 
44: \noindent\textbf{Keywords:} circular cylinder, drag coefficient, towing tank, subcritical Reynolds number, vortex shedding, reproducible research
45: 
46: \section{Introduction}
47: 
48: The flow past a circular cylinder is one of the most studied problems in fluid mechanics, and its drag coefficient is a standard benchmark for experiment, theory and computation \citep{zdravkovich1997,schlichting2017}. At low and moderate Reynolds numbers the boundary layers remain laminar and separate on the rear of the cylinder, producing a broad, unsteady wake and a drag coefficient of order unity. As the Reynolds number approaches the critical value near $2\times10^{5}$, the boundary layers become turbulent, separation is delayed, and the drag coefficient falls sharply before rising again at higher Reynolds numbers \citep{roshko1961,achenbach1968}. The location of this drag crisis, and the drag level on either side of it, depend on surface roughness, free-stream turbulence, aspect ratio and blockage \citep{zdravkovich1997,bearman2011}.
49: 
50: Most textbook drag curves are compilations of measurements from wind tunnels, towing tanks and other facilities, and the scatter between sources at the same Reynolds number is often several percent. Part of this scatter is physical, reflecting differences in roughness and free-stream conditions, and part is experimental, reflecting blockage, support interference, finite aspect ratio and force-balance calibration. Towing tanks are attractive for such measurements because the model can be moved at a controlled speed through still water, the Reynolds number can be varied continuously, and the flow is free of the fan-induced turbulence of a wind tunnel. They also introduce their own difficulties: the model is usually supported from a carriage, the tank walls and free surface constrain the flow, the immersed length is finite, and the load cell is exposed to carriage vibration and to slowly drifting zero offsets.
51: 
52: The present work was motivated by a practical need for a well-documented, reproducible set of towed-cylinder drag measurements at Reynolds numbers between roughly $5\times10^{3}$ and $5\times10^{4}$. This range lies well below the drag crisis and is relevant to small-scale marine and offshore components, to low-speed vehicles, and to the validation of unsteady Reynolds-averaged and large-eddy simulations. The measurements were made over three campaigns in a small tank, with three cylinder diameters and ten speeds per cylinder. The specific aims are: (i) to document the facility, calibration and data-reduction procedure in enough detail that the results can be reproduced; (ii) to quantify the mean drag coefficient and its run-to-run scatter; (iii) to test whether the drag coefficient depends on cylinder diameter at comparable Reynolds numbers; and (iv) to identify the dominant fluctuation frequency in the force signal and use it as an independent check on the flow speed and model geometry.
53: 
54: The paper is deliberately focused on the measurement and its uncertainty rather than on new wake physics. Section~\ref{sec:methods} describes the facility, instrumentation, calibration and data reduction. Section~\ref{sec:results} presents the force records, the drag coefficients and the spectral analysis. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses the systematic uncertainties and their likely effect on the results. Section~\ref{sec:conclusions} summarises the findings and the steps needed before the data are used as a benchmark.
55: 
56: \section{Experimental setup and methods}
57: \label{sec:methods}
58: 
59: \subsection{Facility and models}
60: 
61: The experiments were performed in a towing tank with an internal width of \SI{0.90}{m} and a depth of \SI{0.75}{m}. Three smooth circular cylinders with nominal diameters of \SI{25.4}{mm} (1 in), \SI{38.1}{mm} (1.5 in) and \SI{50.8}{mm} (2 in) were tested. Each model was mounted vertically and immersed to a nominal wetted length of $L=\SI{0.60}{m}$; the same nominal length is used in the drag-coefficient definition for all models. The models were towed at ten nominal speeds between \SI{0.2}{m/s} and \SI{1.1}{m/s} in increments of \SI{0.1}{m/s}. A complete test consisted of \NTotalRuns{} runs: ten for each diameter, except that one run of the \SI{50.8}{mm} model was later excluded (Section~\ref{sec:uncertainty}). The water temperature was recorded for every run and varied between \WaterTempMin{}\si{\celsius} and \WaterTempMax{}\si{\celsius} across the campaign.
62: 
63: The three test campaigns took place in October 2025, November 2025 and March 2026. The load cell was remounted and recalibrated before the March campaign, and the data-acquisition software was updated at the same time. The March files therefore use a semicolon-separated format with columns \texttt{t} and \texttt{V}, whereas the earlier files use a comma-separated format with columns \texttt{time\_s} and \texttt{loadcell\_V}. The processing code detects the format from the file header, so both campaigns are reduced with the same procedure.
64: 
65: \subsection{Force measurement and calibration}
66: 
67: The drag force on the model was measured with a load cell mounted between the model support and the carriage. The bridge voltage was sampled at \SI{200}{Hz} for approximately \SI{12}{s} per run. Each record contains a stationary period before the carriage starts, an acceleration phase, a steady towing phase and a deceleration phase. The steady phase is used for the drag measurement; the acceleration and deceleration phases are discarded because they contain inertial and added-mass contributions.
68: 
69: The load-cell sensitivity was obtained from static calibrations with known masses. The calibration masses were applied in increasing and decreasing order, and the bridge voltage was fitted as a linear function of the applied force,
70: \begin{equation}
71: V = S F + V_{\mathrm{off}},
72: \label{eq:calibration}
73: \end{equation}
74: where $S$ is the sensitivity in \si{V/N} and $V_{\mathrm{off}}$ is the offset. The September 2025 calibration, used for the October and November campaigns, gives $S=\SensSep{}\si{V/N}$ with a standard error of \SensSepRel{}\% and $R^2=\RSqSep{}$. The March 2026 calibration, used for the March campaign, gives $S=\SensMar{}\si{V/N}$ with a standard error of \SensMarRel{}\% and $R^2=\RSqMar{}$. The two sensitivities differ by about 6\%, which is consistent with the load cell having been remounted between campaigns; using a single sensitivity for all data would therefore introduce a systematic error of that order.
75: 
76: For each run, the tare voltage $V_0$ is the mean bridge voltage during the first second of the record, before the carriage starts. The instantaneous force is
77: \begin{equation}
78: F(t) = \frac{V(t)-V_0}{S}.
79: \label{eq:force}
80: \end{equation}
81: The steady-state mean force $\bar F$ is the average of $F(t)$ over the window $3\,\mathrm{s}<t<8\,\mathrm{s}$. This window was chosen to exclude the acceleration transient, which ends at about \SI{2.5}{s}, and the deceleration, which begins at about \SI{8.5}{s} (Fig.~\ref{fig:trace}).
82: 
83: The standard error of $\bar F$ is estimated from ten batch means across the steady window rather than from the standard deviation of the individual samples. The samples are autocorrelated at the vortex-shedding frequency, so the naive standard error would be too small. The batch-means estimate is
84: \begin{equation}
85: \mathrm{s.e.}(\bar F) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{N_b}}\,\mathrm{s.d.}\left(\bar F_1,\ldots,\bar F_{N_b}\right),
86: \label{eq:batch}
87: \end{equation}
88: with $N_b=10$ contiguous batches. This is the uncertainty used for the error bars in Fig.~\ref{fig:cd}.
89: 
90: \subsection{Data reduction}
91: 
92: The drag coefficient is defined using the nominal wetted length $L$ and the cylinder diameter $D$:
93: \begin{equation}
94: C_D = \frac{\bar F}{\tfrac{1}{2}\rho U^2 D L},
95: \label{eq:cd}
96: \end{equation}
97: where $\rho$ is the water density, $U$ is the carriage speed and $L=\SI{0.60}{m}$. The Reynolds number is
98: \begin{equation}
99: Re = \frac{U D}{\nu}, \qquad \nu = \frac{\mu(T)}{\rho(T)}.
100: \label{eq:re}
101: \end{equation}
102: Density and dynamic viscosity are evaluated at the measured water temperature $T$ using standard correlations for air-free water at atmospheric pressure \citep{poling2001,huber2009}. At the temperatures of these experiments the viscosity changes by about 10\% between the coldest and warmest runs, so using a fixed value of $\nu$ would shift the Reynolds number by a similar amount. The drag coefficient itself is much less sensitive to temperature, because $\rho$ changes by less than 0.1\% over the same range.
103: 
104: The uncertainty in $C_D$ from the force measurement is estimated by propagating the batch-means standard error:
105: \begin{equation}
106: \mathrm{s.e.}(C_D) \simeq C_D\,\frac{\mathrm{s.e.}(\bar F)}{\bar F}.
107: \label{eq:cd-unc}
108: \end{equation}
109: This represents the precision of the mean force within a run. It does not include calibration uncertainty, speed uncertainty, blockage or finite-length effects, which are discussed in Section~\ref{sec:uncertainty}.
110: 
111: \subsection{Fluctuation analysis}
112: \label{sec:spectra}
113: 
114: The drag signal is not steady: it contains a periodic component associated with vortex shedding, as well as broadband noise and possible carriage vibration. To characterise this component, the steady window is mean-subtracted and multiplied by a Hann window, and the amplitude spectrum is computed with a fast Fourier transform. The dominant peak above \SI{0.5}{Hz} is located, and its frequency is refined by parabolic interpolation of the three bins around the peak. The frequency resolution of the \SI{5}{s} window is \SI{0.2}{Hz}; the interpolation gives a much finer estimate for the clean, narrowband signals observed here, but the resolution should be kept in mind when interpreting individual runs.
115: 
116: For a circular cylinder, the fluctuating drag is dominated by the second harmonic of the vortex-shedding frequency, because the drag force has two maxima per shedding cycle. The Strouhal number is therefore defined as
117: \begin{equation}
118: St = \frac{f_{\mathrm{peak}} D}{2U},
119: \label{eq:st}
120: \end{equation}
121: where $f_{\mathrm{peak}}$ is the measured peak frequency. If the peak were instead a direct measure of the shedding frequency, the factor of two would be absent; the interpretation is tested in Section~\ref{sec:fluctuations} by comparing the inferred $St$ with the canonical value near 0.2.
122: 
123: \subsection{Uncertainty budget and exclusions}
124: \label{sec:uncertainty}
125: 
126: Table~\ref{tab:summary} summarises the run matrix and the main results. Of the \NTotalRuns{} runs, \NIncludedRuns{} are retained. Run 17, a \SI{50.8}{mm} run at \SI{0.8}{m/s}, is excluded because the run log records that the load-cell zero was suspect after the run. Its drag coefficient is $\RunSeventeenCd$ at $Re=\RunSeventeenRe$, about 18\% above the mean of the other \SI{50.8}{mm} runs. Including it would raise the mean $C_D$ of that cylinder from \MeanCdTwo{} to about 1.18 and would increase the scatter, so it is treated as an outlier and flagged in the processed data rather than silently removed. Run 4, a \SI{25.4}{mm} run at \SI{0.5}{m/s}, was noted as having a small wave left by the previous run; its $C_D$ is within 2\% of the cylinder mean, so it is retained.
127: 
128: The main systematic uncertainties are as follows.
129: 
130: \textit{Blockage.} The tank is \SI{0.90}{m} wide and \SI{0.75}{m} deep. For the largest model the diameter-to-width ratio is \BlockageWidthMax{}\%, and the frontal-area ratio based on the nominal wetted length is \BlockageFrontalMax{}\%. These are large enough that a blockage correction may be needed for a quantitative benchmark. No correction is applied here, because the finite immersed length and the free surface make the standard two-dimensional corrections of uncertain validity. The likely effect is discussed in Section~\ref{sec:systematics}.
131: 
132: \textit{Finite immersed length.} The models are not two-dimensional: the flow can pass beneath the lower end, and the free surface is close to the top of the wetted length. The drag coefficient defined with $L=\SI{0.60}{m}$ therefore includes end effects and may differ from the two-dimensional value. A 5\% error in the effective wetted length would produce a 5\% error in $C_D$.
133: 
134: \textit{Carriage speed.} The nominal carriage speed is used without an independent measurement. A systematic speed error of 2\% would produce a 4\% error in $C_D$, because $C_D\propto U^{-2}$. This is probably the largest single unquantified uncertainty after blockage.
135: 
136: \textit{Tare drift.} The tare is measured only before each run. A slow drift during the run biases the mean force. The exclusion of run 17 addresses the one documented case, but smaller drifts may remain in other runs.
137: 
138: \textit{Free-stream conditions.} The background turbulence and surface waves in the tank were not measured. Both can affect separation and the resulting drag, particularly at the lower Reynolds numbers.
139: 
140: \textit{Calibration.} The calibration slope is known to better than 0.1\% (Section~\ref{sec:methods}), so it is not a leading uncertainty. The remount between campaigns is more important because it changes the sensitivity by about 6\%; this is handled by using the calibration appropriate to each campaign.
