150: \section{Results}
151: \label{sec:results}
152: 
153: \subsection{Force records and fluctuations}
154: \label{sec:fluctuations}
155: 
156: Figure~\ref{fig:trace} shows a representative record, run 5 of the \SI{25.4}{mm} cylinder at \SI{0.6}{m/s}. After the initial transient the force settles to a mean of about \SI{3.10}{N}, with a pronounced oscillation of roughly $\pm\SI{0.2}{N}$, or about 6\% of the mean. The oscillation is narrowband and persists throughout the steady window. Across all retained runs the ratio of the standard deviation to the mean force is between \FstdRelMin{}\% and \FstdRelMax{}\%, with an average of \FstdRelMean{}\%.
157: 
158: \begin{figure}[t]
159: \centering
160: \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figures/paper/fig1_force_trace.pdf}
161: \caption{Representative drag record (run 5: \SI{25.4}{mm} cylinder, $U=\SI{0.6}{m/s}$). The shaded band marks the steady averaging window $3\,\mathrm{s}<t<8\,\mathrm{s}$; the dashed line is the mean over that window. The periodic component is discussed in the text.}
162: \label{fig:trace}
163: \end{figure}
164: 
165: The frequency of the oscillation scales with carriage speed and cylinder diameter. Figure~\ref{fig:repeatability}a plots the inferred Strouhal number, $St=f_{\mathrm{peak}}D/(2U)$, against Reynolds number. All three data sets collapse onto $St=\MeanSt\pm\SdSt$ (standard deviation), with no visible trend over the range $Re=\ReMin$--$\ReMax$. The error bars in Fig.~\ref{fig:repeatability}a show the half-bin frequency resolution of the \SI{5}{s} window, which is the dominant uncertainty at the lowest speeds; the run-to-run standard deviation of the inferred values is \SdSt{}. The factor of two in Eq.~\eqref{eq:st} is the one expected if the drag fluctuation is the second harmonic of vortex shedding; with that interpretation the shedding Strouhal number is \MeanSt{}, close to the canonical value of about 0.2 for a smooth circular cylinder in this Reynolds-number range \citep{norberg2003,williamson1996}. If the measured peak were instead the shedding frequency itself, the implied Strouhal number would be about 0.4, which is not consistent with the cylinder literature. The agreement across 30 runs and three diameters is a useful independent check that the carriage speed and the model diameter are the quantities controlling the fluctuation, and that the oscillation is not a fixed-frequency carriage or load-cell resonance.
166: 
167: \begin{figure}[t]
168: \centering
169: \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figures/paper/fig3_repeatability.pdf}
170: \caption{(a) Strouhal number inferred from the dominant drag fluctuation, $St=f_{\mathrm{peak}}D/(2U)$, versus Reynolds number. The dashed line is $St=0.2$; the error bars show the half-bin frequency resolution of the \SI{5}{s} steady window. (b) Drag coefficient versus run number; the shaded bands mark the three test campaigns. The open red symbol is run 17, excluded because of load-cell zero drift.}
171: \label{fig:repeatability}
172: \end{figure}
173: 
174: \subsection{Drag coefficient}
175: 
176: Figure~\ref{fig:cd} shows $C_D$ against $Re$ for all retained runs, and Table~\ref{tab:summary} gives the group statistics. The overall mean is $\MeanCdAll\pm\SdCdAll$ (standard deviation), with a standard error of the mean of \SemCdAll{}. The individual values range from \CdMin{} to \CdMax{}. The cylinder means are $\MeanCdOne\pm\SdCdOne$ for \SI{25.4}{mm}, $\MeanCdOneFive\pm\SdCdOneFive$ for \SI{38.1}{mm} and $\MeanCdTwo\pm\SdCdTwo$ for \SI{50.8}{mm}. The largest difference between cylinder means is about 1.3\%, which is much smaller than the within-cylinder standard deviations of 0.026--0.034, or 2.3--2.9\% of the mean. Within the resolution of these measurements, therefore, the drag coefficient is independent of cylinder diameter at comparable Reynolds numbers.
177: 
178: \begin{figure}[t]
179: \centering
180: \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figures/paper/fig2_cd_vs_re.pdf}
181: \caption{Drag coefficient versus Reynolds number for the three cylinder diameters. Error bars are the batch-means standard error of the mean force propagated to $C_D$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:cd-unc}); most are smaller than the symbols. The grey band and dashed line show the mean and $\pm1$ standard deviation of all \NIncludedRuns{} retained runs.}
182: \label{fig:cd}
183: \end{figure}
184: 
185: There is a weak increase of $C_D$ with Reynolds number at the lower end of the range. A least-squares fit of $C_D$ against $\log_{10}Re$ gives a slope of $\LogReSlope\pm\LogReSlopeSE$ per decade. The trend is carried mainly by the \SI{25.4}{mm} and \SI{38.1}{mm} data below $Re\approx2\times10^{4}$; the \SI{50.8}{mm} data, which extend to $Re\approx5\times10^{4}$, are essentially flat. This is consistent with a transition from a weakly Reynolds-dependent regime at $Re\lesssim10^{4}$, where $C_D\approx1.10$, to a plateau of $C_D\approx1.15$--$1.16$ at higher Reynolds numbers. The present range does not reach the drag crisis, so no sharp drop in $C_D$ is expected or observed.
186: 
187: The values are at the upper end of the range usually quoted for smooth cylinders in the subcritical regime. Textbooks and compilations give $C_D\approx1.0$--$1.2$ for $10^{3}<Re<2\times10^{5}$, with a representative value near 1.2 \citep{schlichting2017,zdravkovich1997,wieselsberger1921,tritton1959}. The present mean of \MeanCdAll{} is about 4\% below that representative value, which is well within the spread between published data sets and is consistent with the finite immersed length and the modest blockage of the tank. A detailed comparison with a single literature curve is not attempted, because the published curves differ by more than the present run-to-run scatter and because the corrections for blockage and aspect ratio are not applied here.
188: 
189: \subsection{Repeatability}
190: 
191: Figure~\ref{fig:repeatability}b shows $C_D$ in run order, with the three campaigns shaded. There is no visible drift with time: the October, November and March group means agree to within about 1\%, despite the load-cell remount and the change of data-acquisition software between the second and third campaigns. This is reassuring, because a change in sensitivity of about 6\% between campaigns would have produced a clear offset if the wrong calibration had been used.
192: 
193: The run-to-run scatter is \SdCdAll{} overall, or about 2.6\% of the mean. The \SI{50.8}{mm} data show the largest scatter, which is consistent with the larger blockage and the larger absolute force (and therefore the larger effect of any tare drift). Run 17 is a clear outlier at $C_D=\RunSeventeenCd$; the other \SI{50.8}{mm} runs lie between about 1.11 and 1.22. No other run is flagged as an outlier by the criteria used here, although run 4 (noted as following a wave) and run 19 (the highest retained \SI{50.8}{mm} value at 1.215) are worth re-examining if the data are to be used for a formal uncertainty budget.
194: 
195: \section{Discussion}
196: \label{sec:discussion}
197: 
198: \subsection{Interpretation of the drag levels}
199: 
200: The mean drag coefficient of \MeanCdAll{} is consistent with a subcritical, separated flow past a smooth cylinder. The boundary layers are laminar, separation occurs at or slightly aft of the shoulder, and the wake is broad and unsteady. The absence of a drag crisis is expected: the critical Reynolds number for a smooth cylinder is around $2\times10^{5}$, more than a factor of four above the highest Reynolds number reached here. The weak positive Reynolds-number trend at the low end is also plausible. At $Re\approx5\times10^{3}$ the separated shear layers are still relatively thin and the base pressure is less negative than at higher subcritical Reynolds numbers; as $Re$ increases towards $10^{4}$--$10^{5}$, the wake widens and $C_D$ rises towards its subcritical plateau.
201: 
202: The collapse of the fluctuation frequency onto $St\approx0.2$ (with the factor-of-two interpretation) provides a consistency check that is independent of the force calibration. The Strouhal number depends on the ratio of the measured frequency to $U/D$, so a systematic error in the carriage speed or in the diameter would show up as a trend in Fig.~\ref{fig:repeatability}a. No such trend is visible across a factor of ten in Reynolds number and a factor of two in diameter. This does not prove that the absolute speed is correct, because a uniform speed error would shift all points by the same factor, but it does show that the relative speeds and diameters are consistent with the nominal values.
203: 
204: \subsection{Systematic uncertainties and their likely effect}
205: \label{sec:systematics}
206: 
207: The largest systematic uncertainty is blockage. For the \SI{50.8}{mm} model the diameter-to-width ratio is \BlockageWidthMax{}\% and the frontal-area ratio is \BlockageFrontalMax{}\%. A first-order continuity correction would replace the free-stream speed by an effective speed $U/(1-B)$, which would reduce $C_D$ by roughly $2B$ for small $B$; using the frontal-area ratio $B=\BlockageFrontalMax{}\%$ for the largest model, this is of order 9\%. This is an upper bound, because the flow can pass beneath the model and because the wake blockage is not the same as the simple area contraction. A Maskell-type correction \citep{maskell1963}, or the earlier Allen and Vincenti approach \citep{allen1944}, would be more appropriate, but both were developed for two-dimensional bluff bodies in closed wind tunnels and need to be adapted to the finite-length, free-surface geometry here. The fact that the \SI{50.8}{mm} mean is only 1.3\% above the \SI{25.4}{mm} mean suggests that the net blockage effect on the comparison is small, but it could still bias the absolute level by a few percent.
208: 
209: Finite immersed length is the second large uncertainty. The models are not two-dimensional, and the flow around the lower end and near the free surface contributes to the measured force. The nominal wetted length of \SI{0.60}{m} is used without correction for the meniscus, the lower end plate (if any) or the free-surface deformation. A 5\% error in the effective length would change $C_D$ by 5\%, which is larger than the statistical uncertainty of the mean. This is a strong argument for measuring the wetted length directly, or for using end plates and a deeper immersion, in any follow-up experiment.
210: 
211: Carriage speed is the third. The nominal speed is set by the carriage controller, but no independent encoder or laser measurement is available in the present data set. A 2\% speed error would produce a 4\% error in $C_D$. This uncertainty is correlated across all runs at the same nominal speed and would not average out with more repeats.
212: 
213: Tare drift and free-stream conditions are harder to quantify. The exclusion of run 17 removes the one documented zero problem, but smaller drifts may remain. The tank is small enough that waves generated by one run can persist into the next; the run log notes such a wave for run 4, although its effect on $C_D$ is within the scatter. Free-stream turbulence was not measured, and it can change the separation point and the drag level, especially near the lower end of the Reynolds-number range.
214: 
215: \subsection{Implications for small-tank measurements}
216: 
217: The results suggest that a small towing tank can produce drag coefficients within about 5\% of the canonical subcritical values, provided that the load cell is calibrated in situ, the tare is checked before and after each run, and the water temperature is recorded. The precision of an individual run is much better than 5\%: the batch-means standard error of the mean force is typically well below 1\%, and the run-to-run scatter of about 3\% is dominated by slowly varying effects such as tare drift, waves and blockage rather than by random noise. For benchmark purposes, therefore, repeated runs and a formal uncertainty budget are more valuable than a single high-resolution record.
218: 
219: The data are also a useful test case for reproducibility workflows. The raw files, calibration files, run log, processing scripts, figures and manuscript are all part of one Calkit project. The pipeline processes the two DAQ formats, regenerates the processed table and the figures, and compiles this document. A reader can therefore trace every number in the paper to a raw file and a processing step, and can rerun the analysis after changing, for example, the averaging window or the viscosity correlation. This is a modest but practical contribution: many small experimental data sets are published as a table of final values without the intermediate processing, which makes it difficult to assess the effect of analysis choices.
220: 
221: \subsection{Limitations and future work}
222: 
223: Several limitations should be addressed before the data are used as a formal benchmark. First, a blockage correction appropriate to the finite-length, free-surface geometry should be developed or adapted, and the corrected and uncorrected values should both be reported. Second, the effective wetted length should be measured, and end effects should be quantified, for example by repeating a subset of runs with different immersion depths or with end plates. Third, the carriage speed should be measured independently, ideally with an encoder or a laser, so that the speed uncertainty can be separated from the drag uncertainty. Fourth, the tare should be recorded before and after every run, which would allow linear drift to be removed rather than merely flagged. Fifth, the background turbulence and surface waves should be characterised, at least qualitatively, and the time between runs should be long enough for the water to settle.
224: 
225: On the modelling side, the data are well suited to a comparison with unsteady Reynolds-averaged Navier--Stokes or large-eddy simulations at the same Reynolds numbers, provided that the blockage and finite-length geometry are included in the computational domain. The Strouhal-number result gives an additional target for such simulations, and the force records provide the fluctuation amplitude as well as the mean.
226: 
227: \section{Conclusions}
228: \label{sec:conclusions}
229: 
230: Drag measurements have been made for three smooth circular cylinders towed through a small tank at subcritical Reynolds numbers. The main findings are:
231: 
232: \begin{enumerate}
233: \item Over \NIncludedRuns{} retained runs, the mean drag coefficient is $\MeanCdAll\pm\SdCdAll$ (standard deviation), with a standard error of the mean of \SemCdAll{}. The cylinder means are \MeanCdOne{}, \MeanCdOneFive{} and \MeanCdTwo{} for diameters of \SI{25.4}{mm}, \SI{38.1}{mm} and \SI{50.8}{mm}, respectively.
234: \item The differences between cylinder means are smaller than the run-to-run scatter, so no resolvable dependence on diameter is found over the range $Re=\ReMin$--$\ReMax$.
235: \item The drag coefficient increases weakly with Reynolds number at the lower end of the range, with a fitted slope of $\LogReSlope\pm\LogReSlopeSE$ per decade, consistent with the approach to the subcritical plateau.
236: \item The dominant periodic component of the drag signal collapses onto $St=\MeanSt\pm\SdSt$ when interpreted as the second harmonic of vortex shedding. This provides an independent check on the speed and diameter scaling and confirms that the fluctuations are flow-induced rather than a fixed carriage resonance.
237: \item One run (run 17) is excluded because of load-cell zero drift; its drag coefficient is about 18\% above the mean of the other runs at the same diameter.
238: \item Blockage (up to \BlockageWidthMax{}\% of the tank width), finite immersed length, and unmeasured carriage-speed uncertainty are the leading systematic uncertainties. They are not corrected here and should be addressed in a formal benchmark.
239: \end{enumerate}
